Stage 01: offline package validation only. No rules are enabled or evaluated.

The selected, unmodified SigmaHQ rules remain in the separately obtained archive
identified by lock.json. The archive hash identifies the exact ZIP used by the
design; GitHub may regenerate ZIP bytes for the same commit. A different ZIP
must be reviewed and relocked, never silently accepted. SHA-256 integrity does
not authenticate the publisher. Obtain packages from the official pinned URL
or a trusted offline channel.

DRL-1.1: retain original YAML bytes, author/attribution metadata, rule and set
links, and the license notice/text when distributing rules. The verifier keeps
original bytes and attribution in memory and never prints author values.
Future messages based on matches (including cards, API, exports, notifications,
and aggregated chains) must retain the supplied authorship of every input rule.
Local modifications must be identified separately, preserving upstream credit.
See licenses/DRL-1.1.txt and the pinned license URI in lock.json.

pySigma 1.3.3 is LGPL-2.1-only, independently of the rules' DRL license.
See licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt and the source URI in lock.json. This stage does not
bundle the library into the server. Any later distribution must preserve the
library's license/copyright notices, provide the corresponding library source
as required by LGPL-2.1, and allow replacement/modification of the library.
Keep it separately installed; do not merge or freeze it into proprietary code.
Beta 1 excludes Sigma. The optional pySigma installation and its diskcache
and diskcache-stubs dependencies have been removed from requirements.lock.txt
and software-manifest.json. Remaining ancillary packages retain their hashes.
The parser version and license in lock.json describe the historical verifier;
this lock no longer installs that parser. Do not install it transitively to
restore the retired tool environment. Package integrity checks are unchanged.

The archive is an external input, not committed rule content or runtime data.
Unit fixtures are synthetic and require no network or database. The optional
SLI_SIGMA_ARCHIVE test additionally parses all 25 original pinned YAML files.
